// @error no field 'getF32' on I32View
// THE ELEMENT TYPE IS PART OF THE TYPE, AND `new` IS WHAT MAKES IT SO.
//
// VL is STRUCTURALLY typed: a plain `type X = {…}` names a shape, not a nominal
// identity, so two declarations with the same field names and types are THE SAME
// TYPE and are silently interchangeable. `F32View` and `I32View` are both
// `{ base: i32, length: i32 }` — spelled WITHOUT `new` this program COMPILES and
// `id.getF32(0)` reinterprets four integer bytes as a float with no diagnostic.
// Measured, not assumed: the inverted control is this same std with `new` deleted
// from the two declarations, and it accepts this file.
//
// So both views are declared `new` (webcraft P1.5,
// `docs/internals/newtype-design.md`). The identity belongs to the TYPE, not to a
// field name, which is why the address field is plainly `base` in both.
//
// ZERO-COST: a newtype is erased before the emitter runs. Every correct view
// program in this directory emits BYTE-IDENTICALLY against the same std with
// `new` deleted. And because the two views are now genuinely one shape, they
// share ONE wasm heap type where the `f32base`/`i32base` spelling needed two —
// so restoring the safety made every view program 12 bytes SMALLER.
//
// The error names the ALIAS because a newtype gives `tyToStr` a name to render.
// The predecessor of this pin read `no field 'getF32' on {i32base: i32, length:
// i32}`, a structural render — itself the evidence that no nominal identity
// existed to name.
import { Buffer, getF32, i32view } from "std:buffer"

const b = Buffer(64)
const id = b.i32view(0, 4)
const bad = id.getF32(0)
print(bad)
